ma\\hat{k}$$\n\nWait: $\\vec{V}_\\infty \\times \\Gamma\\hat{k} = U\\hat{x} \\times \\Gamma\\hat{z} = U\\Gamma(\\hat{x}\\times\\hat{z}) = U\\Gamma(-\\hat{y}) = -U\\Gamma\\hat{y}$\n\nSo $\\vec{F} = \\rho(-U\\Gamma\\hat{y}) = -\\rho U\\Gamma\\hat{y}$. Same result. \u2713\n\nOr equivalently: $\\vec{F} = -\\rho \\vec{V}_\\infty \\times (\\Gamma\\hat{k})$... no, that would give the opposite sign.\n\nLet me just use: $\\vec{F} = \\rho \\vec{V}_\\infty \\times \\vec{\\Gamma}$ where $\\vec{\\Gamma} = \\Gamma\\hat{z}$.\n\n$\\vec{V}_\\infty \\times \\vec{\\Gamma} = U\\hat{x} \\times \\Gamma\\hat{z} = -U\\Gamma\\hat{y}$\n\n$\\vec{F} = -\\rho U\\Gamma\\hat{y}$ \u2713\n\nSo the correct Kutta-Joukowski formula gives $\\vec{F} = -\\rho U\\Gamma\\hat{y}$, which agrees with my direct integration.\n\nMy earlier error was in the sign of the cross product. $\\hat{x} \\times \\hat{z} = -\\hat{y}$, not $+\\hat{y}$.\n\nGreat, so everything is consistent. The force per unit length is:\n$$\\vec{F} = -\\rho U\\Gamma\\,\\hat{y}$$\n\nor in components: $F_x = 0$, $F_y = -\\rho U\\Gamma$.\n\nFor $\\Gamma > 0$ (counterclockwise), the force is in the $-\\hat{y}$ direction (downward).\nFor $\\Gamma < 0$ (clockwise), the force is in the $+\\hat{y}$ direction (upward).\n\nThis makes physical sense: counterclockwise circulation means the flow on top is slower (pressure higher) and on the bottom is faster (pressure lower), giving a net downward force.\n\nLet me now write up the complete solution cleanly.\n\nActually, I want to present the pressure in a slightly different form. Let me use:\n\n$$p(a,\\theta) = p_\\infty + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho U^2(1 - 2\\sin^2\\theta) + \\frac{\\rho U\\Gamma}{\\pi a}\\sin\\theta - \\frac{\\rho\\Gamma^2}{8\\pi^2 a^2}$$\n\nThis can also be written as:\n$$p(a,\\theta) = p_\\infty + \\frac{\\rho U^2}{2}\\cos 2\\theta - \\frac{\\rho U^2}{2} + \\frac{\\rho U\\Gamma}{\\pi a}\\sin\\theta - \\frac{\\rho\\Gamma^2}{8\\pi^2 a^2}$$\n\nI'll present both forms.\n\nLet me now write the fina